The Meta MMS alignment model is not part of this repository or covered by the
GPL. The apps download it separately when it is first needed. It is licensed
under CC-BY-NC 4.0, which does not allow commercial use.

The ZipVoice-Distill speech synthesis model (k2-fsa/ZipVoice) and Vocos 24 kHz
vocoder (charactr/vocos-mel-24khz) are likewise downloaded separately for on-device
reading and zero-shot voice cloning. ZipVoice's code is Apache-2.0 and its
pre-trained weights (trained on the Emilia corpus) are licensed under CC-BY-NC 4.0
(non-commercial use only); Vocos is licensed under MIT.

The built-in voice card pack (voices-1) is generated entirely from text descriptions
using Qwen3-TTS VoiceDesign with no human voice recordings, and is dedicated to the
public domain under CC0 1.0.

The sound library (sounds-1) is generated with MOSS-SoundEffect v2.0 (Apache-2.0)
from text descriptions and is dedicated to the public domain under CC0 1.0.

On macOS, the optional studio speech models (Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B Base and VoiceDesign,
quantized via MLX) are downloaded separately when requested by the user and are
licensed under Apache-2.0.

The 3D-Speaker CAM++ speaker-embedding model (iic/speech_campplus_sv_zh-cn_16k-common)
is likewise downloaded separately, for telling voices apart in radio plays. It is
licensed under Apache-2.0. scripts/align-bench/export_speaker.py converts it; the model
definition in that script is ported from 3D-Speaker (speakerlab/models/campplus,
Copyright 3D-Speaker, Apache-2.0).

On Android, the SenseVoice-Small speech recognition model (FunAudioLLM, as exported
by sherpa-onnx) is downloaded separately to transcribe recordings and check radio
play clips. It is licensed under the FunASR Model License Agreement
(https://github.com/modelscope/FunASR/blob/main/MODEL_LICENSE), which asks that its
source, authors and model name be credited.

